From aab34e05aa67a44fe6c21c9dc0cba422e6276150 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:19:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 13/32] thread: back the global critical region with a
 recursive SRW lock on Windows

Generated code enters the global critical region around every guest
critical section (REX_ENTER_GLOBAL_LOCK in pch_h.inja), and the kernel
takes it for every object table lookup and shared memory request, so it
is one of the most frequently acquired locks in the runtime. On Windows
it was a std::recursive_mutex, whose lock and unlock go through the C++
runtime DLL (msvcp140, which under Wine may be Wine's own build).

Xenia Canary replaced its global std::recursive_mutex on Windows with
xe_global_mutex (src/xenia/base/mutex.h, mutex.cc): an exclusive SRW
lock plus an owner thread id and a recursion count. Port it as
global_mutex_type. The SRWLOCK is stored as its one pointer-sized field
so the public header needs no windows.h, and the owner id is a relaxed
atomic: another thread reading it races with the owner's store, but can
only ever see its own id while it holds the lock. Other platforms keep
std::recursive_mutex.

The lock type of global_critical_region::Acquire*() becomes
global_unique_lock_type, and the watch and access violation callbacks
that receive the held lock take that type.

Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
